    summary: `Bayard Rustin helped organize some of the most visible events of the civil-rights movement while being pushed out of view. He was a pacifist, labor ally, strategist, singer, Quaker-influenced organizer, and openly gay Black man in a movement and country that often treated his sexuality as a vulnerability. Rustin's life asks readers to look behind the microphone. Mass marches do not organize themselves. Nonviolence does not become strategy by accident. Coalitions do not appear because a poster says unity. Rustin built logistics, trained activists, connected movements, and paid the price for being indispensable in public work but inconvenient in public image.`,
